C = [
("h1", "7. 1 Corinthians 9: The Right Laid Down"),
("p", "Claim: 1 Corinthians 9 is Paul’s own case of a right he has and does not use. The chapter sits between the limit on liberty in chapter 8 and the warning in chapter 10, and it shows what the limit looks like in a life. It is not a new rule for meat. It is Paul’s account of how he conducts himself where he is free."),
("sub", "7.1 The rights Paul has"),
("p", "Paul begins with questions: “Am I not an apostle? am I not free? have I not seen Jesus Christ our Lord? are not ye my work in the Lord?” (1 Corinthians 9:1). He then names the rights he has as an apostle. “Have we not power to eat and to drink?” (1 Corinthians 9:4). “Have we not power to lead about a sister, a wife, as well as other apostles, and as the brethren of the Lord, and Cephas?” (1 Corinthians 9:5). “Or I only and Barnabas, have not we power to forbear working?” (1 Corinthians 9:6). The rights are real. He supports them from common practice (a soldier, a vinedresser, a shepherd, 9:7), from “the law of Moses” (“Thou shalt not muzzle the mouth of the ox that treadeth out the corn,” 9:9), and from the Lord’s ordinance: “Even so hath the Lord ordained that they which preach the gospel should live of the gospel” (1 Corinthians 9:14)."),
("sub", "7.2 The rights not used"),
("p", "“Nevertheless we have not used this power; but suffer all things, lest we should hinder the gospel of Christ” (1 Corinthians 9:12). “But I have used none of these things” (1 Corinthians 9:15). The reason is the gospel. What is at stake is not food, and Paul does not say that his rights are wrong. He says that he lays them down so that he may “make the gospel of Christ without charge, that I abuse not my power in the gospel” (1 Corinthians 9:18). The pattern is the same as chapter 8: a right, a reason to lay it down, and a person for whose sake it is laid down. The person there is the weak brother; the person here is the one to whom Paul is preaching."),
("sub", "7.3 Servant to all"),
("p", "“For though I be free from all men, yet have I made myself servant unto all, that I might gain the more” (1 Corinthians 9:19). Paul describes what the servant’s course looks like. “And unto the Jews I became as a Jew, that I might gain the Jews; to them that are under the law, as under the law, that I might gain them that are under the law; to them that are without law, as without law, (being not without law to God, but under the law to Christ,) that I might gain them that are without law. To the weak became I as weak, that I might gain the weak: I am made all things to all men, that I might by all means save some” (1 Corinthians 9:20–22). Four classes are named: the Jews, those under the law, those without law and the weak. The word “as” runs through the sentence."),
("p", "The volume reads the sentence with Paul’s commission in view. Paul’s commission never changes: he is the apostle of the Gentiles, and his own gospel is the gospel of the grace of God. When he becomes as a Jew to Jews, he adapts his manner to his hearers, as the passage itself says he does. That is audience-specific conduct, not a second gospel and not a change of commission, and it does not place Paul under the law or inside Israel’s program. Paul’s synagogue preaching was audience-specific, not commission-specific. The KJV has Paul say that he became “as under the law” to those under it; it does not say in this sentence that he was under it, and the volume does not add the words. What Acts reports of his conduct among Jews, he circumcising Timothy “because of the Jews which were in those quarters” (Acts 16:3), the vow at Cenchrea (Acts 18:18), the purification in the temple (Acts 21:26), is reported as written. The reasons are given in the narrative, in the places and for the persons named. They are not made rules for the churches."),
("sub", "7.4 The weak, once more"),
("p", "“To the weak became I as weak, that I might gain the weak” (1 Corinthians 9:22). The word is the same as the weak brother of chapter 8. Paul does not say who the weak are here, and the volume does not infer it. The sentence tells what Paul does for them: he becomes as weak, and his reason is to gain them. The reading gives chapter 8 a person. The strong man who lays down his right for the weak is doing what Paul did."),
("sub", "7.5 The race and the body"),
("p", "The chapter ends in an athlete’s image. “Know ye not that they which run in a race run all, but one receiveth the prize? So run, that ye may obtain” (1 Corinthians 9:24). “And every man that striveth for the mastery is temperate in all things” (1 Corinthians 9:25). “But I keep under my body, and bring it into subjection: lest that by any means, when I have preached to others, I myself should be a castaway” (1 Corinthians 9:27). The volume reads the passage as Paul’s account of self-control for the sake of his work. It reports the last word, “castaway,” as the KJV has it, and does not build on it a doctrine of loss of salvation or of reward, since the verse says only what Paul fears for himself in relation to his preaching and does not say what he would lose."),
("ctbl", "1 Corinthians 9 by the five questions.", [
    ("Question", "Answer from the passage", "Verse"),
    ("Does Paul command?", "No; he gives his own conduct as the example", "9:12, 15, 19"),
    ("What limit?", "A right laid down for the gospel’s sake", "9:12, 18"),
    ("To whom is it owed?", "To those to whom he preaches, and to the weak", "9:19–22"),
    ("What ground?", "That the gospel not be hindered; that he may gain the more", "9:12, 19, 23"),
    ("What is unsaid?", "Whether others must lay down the same rights; what “castaway” loses", "Chapter 16"),
  ], [0.26, 0.52, 0.22]),
("sub", "7.6 What the volume takes from the chapter"),
("bul", [
    "Paul has real rights and lays them down for the gospel’s sake. The limit is a laying down, and it is his choice.",
    "His becoming all things to all men is audience-specific conduct, not a change of commission or of gospel.",
    "He becomes as weak to the weak, and the volume does not say who the weak were beyond what the text says.",
    "The chapter gives no rule for others. It gives an example.",
  ]),
]
